sec.p:
Ironically, colours are no longer needed: the digits encode the colour information of the nodes on the spine; the trees hanging from the spine are black anyway. The resulting structure is also known as a (one-sided) finger tree — the spine view can be seen as a finger pointing to the leftmost element. Of course, we have come across the Spine datatype before. The left-spine view is a special case of a context: if ( 𝛾,
Leaf
) is the location of the leftmost leaf, then 𝛾 represents the left spine. In more detail, we have the following correspondences.

On an amusing note, recall that the spine is a special case of a zipper, so we are “zippifying” a zipper. One-hole contexts have been characterized as derivatives of datatypes [
8
]. Hence, our new zipper datatype is a second derivative. Zipping a typed context with a typed term corresponds closely to function application — hence the name of the function.
